%U A397916 63764379497504341713680,245422660645537137035294192,1785484430721093648925894813952,24621708450182126364426665394856672,645255958743928033669012765494617460096
%N A397916 Number of equivalence classes of n X n {-1,1} matrices that are equivalent to their transposes under independent permutations of rows and columns and multiplication of rows and columns by -1.
%C A397916 Two matrices A and B are equivalent if B = P*A*Q for signed permutation matrices P and Q.
%C A397916 The sequence counts equivalence classes [A] satisfying [A] = [A^T].
%C A397916 Among the equivalence classes counted by A352099(n), exactly a(n) are invariant under transposition. Allowing transposition as an additional equivalence operation gives A353052(n).
%H A397916 Vladeta Jovovic, <a href="/A397916/b397916.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..87</a>
%F A397916 a(0) = 1. For n >= 1, a(n) = Sum_{lambda partition of n} 2^E(lambda)/z_lambda, where lambda = (lambda_1, ..., lambda_l), z_lambda = Product_{k >= 1} k^m_k * m_k!, and m_k is the multiplicity of k in lambda.
%F A397916 E(lambda) = (n + o(lambda))/2 - l + Sum_{1 <= i < j <= l} gcd(lambda_i, lambda_j) + epsilon(lambda), where o(lambda) is the number of odd parts of lambda, and epsilon(lambda) = 1 if o(lambda) = 0, and 0 otherwise.
%F A397916 a(n) = 2*A353052(n) - A352099(n) for n >= 1.
%p A397916 For n = 3, let A = [-1, 1, -1; 1, 1, 1; 1, 1, -1]. To obtain A^T, multiply the first row by -1, interchange the first two rows, interchange the first two columns, and then multiply the first column by -1. Thus [A] = [A^T].
%Y A397916 Cf. A352099, A353052.
%K A397916 nonn,new
%O A397916 0,3
